<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Andoche Junot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1771">1771</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Duke of Abrantes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Andoche Junot was a French general and early comrade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>. In November 1807 he led the invasion of Portugal, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> just after the royal court had sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, and was made Duke of Abrantes. Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Arthur Wellesley</a> at Vimeiro in August 1808, he evacuated Portugal under the Convention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sintra">Sintra</a>. He later served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and died in 1813.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-andoche-junot.jpg" alt="Jean-Andoche Junot" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea Appiani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Junot,_par_Appiani.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Junot was born on 25 September 1771 in Bussy-le-Grand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, the fifth son of a bourgeois family. He studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> and was a law clerk in Chaumont when the Revolution began. He enlisted in 1791 as a grenadier of the Volunteers of Côte-d'Or and received the first of many wounds at La Glisuelle in 1792, earning the nickname la Tempête, the Tempest. At the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> in late 1793 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> by volunteering to write a letter for him, and became his aide-de-camp. He followed Bonaparte to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where at Lonato he killed six enemy soldiers in hand-to-hand fighting, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was promoted brigade general on 9 January 1799.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Junot married Laure Martin de Permond in 1800, was made a general of division in 1801, served as Military Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1803 and 1804 and fought at Austerlitz on 2 December 1805. On 29 July 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> appointed him to command the Gironde Observation Corps for the invasion of Portugal. He left on 17 October with about 26,500 soldiers, crossed into Portugal on 19 November and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> without a fight on 30 November, three days after the royal family's departure. Napoleon gave him the title Duke of Abrantes but not the rank of Marshal he expected. From late December 1807 he disbanded the Portuguese Army, with about 9,000 soldiers joining the Portuguese Legion, and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a> dethroned.</p><p>Revolts spread in 1808, the Spanish troops withdrew and a British force landed on 1 August. After a French defeat at Roliça, Junot was beaten at Vimeiro on 21 August, and the Convention of Cintra let him return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with the army's weapons, baggage and loot. It caused widespread outrage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He then fought at the Second Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and, in 1810, under Marshal Masséna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1811 Junot was shot in the face, and complications of the wound were blamed for his later mental decline. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812 he was blamed for letting the Russian army retreat after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, which led to his removal from command, although he commanded the VIII Corps competently at Borodino. Made Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> in May 1813, he deteriorated rapidly and was relieved and sent to his father's house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. He died of an infection in Montbard on 29 July 1813 after injuring himself. He is buried in Montbard cemetery, where a monument was erected in 1898, and his title passed to his sons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean-Andoche Junot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1807, as Napoleon’s army under <b>Junot</b> advanced, the royal court sailed to Rio de Janeiro, and in 1815 the realm became the United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bussy-le-Grand, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Montbard, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.57,4.52&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Bussy-le-Grand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jean-Andoche Junot"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5260 6045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5260 6045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.57,4.52&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
